course: world
lesson: 162
title: Who stole the remote? Jumping to conclusions at home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Dad can't find the TV remote. "Right, who's taken it? Ruby, you were watching cartoons." Ruby was at school all day, and the remote is in Dad's own dressing-gown pocket. He picked a suspect before he looked for a single clue.
explain: That is jumping to conclusions: deciding the answer first and checking afterwards, or never. At home it happens all the time: a crash in the kitchen, mud on the carpet, an empty biscuit tin, a charger that "someone broke".
explain: Good detectives do it the other way round. They check the easy things first (your own pockets, your own head, the plug switch), then ask simple questions: who was there, who could even reach it, what do the clues look like?
explain: The usual suspect is a guess, not proof. Sam "always" eats the biscuits, but this time Dad ate eight of the ten. The muddy prints in the hall are paw-shaped, with toes, so it is the dog, not Noah in his socks.
explain: The family takes the mickey out of the jump, not the person: "Check your pocket, Sherlock." Saying sorry when the clue points the other way is how a fair grown-up ends it.
fact: Jumping to conclusions means deciding what happened before looking at the evidence.
fact: Most "stolen" things at home are just lost or put down somewhere and forgotten.
fact: Check yourself first: your own pockets, bag and head are the quickest places to look.
fact: What someone did last time is a clue to check, not proof of what happened this time.
fact: "He always does it" is a guess; counting what really happened is evidence.
fact: When something doesn't work, test the simple causes first: is the socket switched on, is the cable in? The boring answer is usually right.
fact: Look at all the clues, including ones that point away from your suspect, like paw prints with toes.
fact: Ask "could they even have done it?": a two-year-old cannot zip up a schoolbag, and someone at school cannot take a remote at home.
fact: Being near something when it breaks does not mean you broke it, and an accident is not the same as doing it on purpose.
fact: Tyres often get punctured by thorns and nails, so look at the tyre before looking for a villain.
fact: Accusing someone of stealing is serious and hurts them if it is wrong, so you need evidence before saying it out loud.
fact: Words people use: "nicked" means stole (UK), "Sherlock" is a teasing name for someone playing detective badly, "sus" means suspicious, "no cap" means honestly, "it's giving charcoal" means it looks like charcoal (young slang), "sass" means cheek, "ye wee rascal" is a fond Scottish telling-off, and "resting his eyes" is a joke for having a nap.
exercise: Dad blames Ruby for the missing remote because she watches cartoons. Where is the remote? | In Dad's own dressing-gown pocket | In Ruby's school bag | Down the back of the sofa | In the dog's basket
exercise: What was wrong with Dad's reason for blaming Ruby? | It fits everyone who watches TV, and she was at school all day | He should have blamed the cat instead | He didn't shout loud enough for her to hear | Ruby had taken the remote the day before as well, so it must be her again
exercise: Mum hears a crash and shouts "JAKE!", but Jake is in the bath. What made the crash? | The cat knocked a plate off the side | Jake sneaking out of the bath to play | The wind blowing the kitchen window open | Dad dropping a plate and then hiding it
exercise: Ava says Leo popped her tyre on purpose. What was really in it? | A thorn | A pin Leo pushed in | Nothing, the tyre was fine | A crack from the cold
exercise: Gran says somebody moved her glasses, and everyone searches for twenty minutes. Where are they? | On her head | In the fridge | In the grandson's pocket | Under her chair
exercise: Dad blames Sam for the empty biscuit tin. Who ate most of them? | Dad, who ate eight while watching football | Sam, who ate all ten | The dog, who found the tin | Mum, who took them to work
exercise: Mum says someone broke her charger. What was the real problem? | The socket was switched off | The cable was chewed by the dog | Somebody had hidden the plug | The phone was too old to charge
exercise: What is the best first step when something at home "doesn't work"? | Test the simple things, like the switch and the cable | Ask who touched it last, and then blame them until they say sorry | Buy a new one straight away | Shout until someone owns up
exercise: Mud on the hall carpet is in the shape of paws, and Noah is in his socks. Who made the mess? | The dog | Noah | Noah's friend from next door | Dad coming back from work
exercise: Uncle Dev says the window cleaner took his wallet. Where is it? | Under the car seat | In the window cleaner's bucket | In the kitchen drawer | In Auntie's handbag
exercise: Lily blames two-year-old Max for her missing homework. Where was it? | In her own schoolbag, which she zipped up | Torn up in Max's toy box | Taken to school by Mum | Blown out of the window
exercise: Dad finds the TV on in an "empty" room and blames the kids. Who left it on? | Grandad, asleep in the armchair round the corner | The kids, before they went out | Nobody at all, because the TV turned itself on in the night | Mum, who forgot to switch it off
exercise: Mum smells burning and asks who's been playing with matches. What is burning? | Her own forgotten toast | A candle in the kids' room | Something in the garden | A plug that's caught fire
exercise: What do grown-ups call deciding who did it before looking at the evidence? | Jumping to conclusions | Thinking ahead | Detective work | Being sensible
exercise: Why should you have evidence before saying someone stole something? | Being wrongly accused is serious and hurts | Because thieves always own up anyway | Because evidence makes the shouting louder | Because only police may ask questions
exercise: Which question would have cleared Lily's brother Max quickly? | Could a two-year-old even reach the table and work a zip? | Has Max been naughty this week? | Does Max like homework? | Was Max cross with Lily yesterday?
test: Ruby: "Yeah, I nicked it from school using my mind powers, Dad." What is she pointing out? | She was at school, so she couldn't have taken it | She really does have mind powers | She took it with her before she left for school that morning | She thinks Dad stole it from her
test: Jake from the bath: "Unless I've learned to teleport, it wasn't me, innit." What does he mean? | He was in the bath, so he couldn't be in the kitchen | He has learned to teleport | He broke the plate earlier, just before he got into his bath | He doesn't know what teleport means
test: Leo: "Oh yeah, I spent my whole Saturday hunting thorns. That's my hobby now." What is he teasing? | The jump from "I rode it" to "I did it on purpose" | His sister for owning a bike | The silly idea that thorns could ever grow on a pavement | His own bad riding
test: Grandson, trying not to laugh: "Gran, you've been wearing the suspect the whole time." Gran: "Don't stand there smirking, ye wee rascal." What is true? | The glasses were on Gran's head all along | The grandson had hidden the glasses as a joke | Gran was really, truly angry with her grandson | The glasses were stolen and then put back later
test: Sam: "I had two, you had eight, and I'm the criminal? You're so sus, Dad." What trap did Dad fall into? | Blaming the usual suspect without counting | Eating too few biscuits | Buying too few biscuits | Not hiding the biscuit tin well enough from the kids
test: What would have happened if Mum had tried another plug first? | She'd have found the charger worked and nobody broke it | She'd have found that the charger was really broken after all | Both plugs would have stopped working | She'd still have needed a new phone
test: Noah: "Mum, unless I walk on four legs, it's the dog. They've got toes!" Is he being rude? | No, he is mocking the reasoning, and the clue is good | Yes, he is calling Mum a dog | Yes, he should take the blame anyway | No, he is just trying to blame his little sister for it instead
test: Auntie: "Unless he's got a very long arm, check the car." What does she mean? | The window cleaner was in another street, so look elsewhere first | The window cleaner has very long arms | Dev left his wallet on a car roof | Window cleaners always hide the wallets they take inside people's cars
test: A cartoon detective decides the butler did it in the first second, and is always right. What is wrong with that in real life? | Real guesses need checking; the first suspect is often wrong | Butlers really do cause most crimes | Detectives never use clues in real life | Guessing fast always finds the right person
test: Teen son: "It's Grandad, he's snoring louder than the telly. Bro's been resting his eyes since lunch." What was Dad's mistake? | Saying it was the kids as a fact before looking round | Leaving the telly on himself | Waking Grandad up | Paying for electricity
test: Kid: "Mum, it's your toast. It's black. It's giving charcoal." What is the lesson? | Check what is actually burning before deciding who | Kids should never be in the kitchen | Toast always burns in the end, so it's best not to make any at all | Charcoal is made from toast
exam: Identify the principal deficiency in attributing the disappearance of a remote control to a child who was at school throughout the day. | The accusation preceded any examination of the evidence | The child had insufficient interest in television | The parent omitted to search the garden and the shed before accusing | The remote control was of an unusual design
exam: Determine the most appropriate initial course of action upon discovering that a device fails to charge. | Verification of the simplest causes, such as whether the socket is switched on | Immediate accusation of the person who last used it | Purchase of a replacement charger without delay | Interrogation of all members of the household
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning footprints displaying toes upon a hall carpet while the accused child wears socks. | The evidence indicates the dog rather than the child | The evidence confirms the child's guilt | The evidence is irrelevant to the enquiry | The evidence indicates an intruder who walked in wearing heavy boots
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the claim that a person "always" commits a certain act? | It is a presumption, not evidence concerning the present case | It constitutes conclusive proof of guilt | It is a legal finding, and is therefore binding upon the whole household | It is a measurement obtained by counting
